            <ArticleTitle>MicroRNA-205 inhibits cancer cell migration and invasion via modulation of centromere protein F regulating pathways in prostate cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>867-77</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/iju.12829</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the functional roles of microRNA-205 in the modulation of novel cancer pathways in prostate cancer cells.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Functional studies of microRNA-205 were carried out to investigate cell proliferation, migration and invasion in prostate cancer cell lines (PC3 and DU145) by restoration of mature microRNA. In silico database and genome-wide gene expression analyses were carried out to identify molecular targets and pathways mediated by microRNA-205. Loss-of-function studies were applied to microRNA-205 target genes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Restoration of microRNA-205 in cancer cell lines significantly inhibited cancer cell migration and invasion. Our data showed that the centromere protein F gene was overexpressed in prostate cancer clinical specimens and was a direct target of microRNA-205 regulation. Silencing of centromere protein F significantly inhibited cancer cell migration and invasion. Furthermore, MCM7, an oncogenic gene functioning downstream of centromere protein F, was identified by si-centromere protein F transfectants in prostate cancer cells.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Loss of tumor-suppressive microRNA-205 seems to enhance cancer cell migration and invasion in prostate cancer through direct regulation of centromere protein F. Our data describing pathways regulated by tumor-suppressive microRNA-205 provide new insights into the potential mechanisms of prostate cancer oncogenesis and metastasis.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 The Japanese Urological Association.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
